# Plane-host cookie bounce (ALB → Lambda → middle) — validated design & way forward

**Status:** validated end to end.

- Bounce mechanics on real AWS (ALB + Lambda + IAM/STS on `ishentras.click`) and locally (ALB emulator).
- **GitLab OAuth consent (Coder external auth) with a real gitlab.com OAuth app**, locally: portal →
  plane host → GitLab → Coder → back to the portal, with the consent session **revoked and the cookie
  cleared** at the end (the user can't reuse it to browse the Coder dashboard).

Labs torn down; code lives in this repo.

## 1. Problem

Some Coder pages authenticate **only with the `coder_session_token` cookie on the plane host**:

| Page | Why a query token doesn't work |
| --- | --- |
| External-auth consent `/external-auth/{id}` and its callback `/external-auth/{id}/callback` (GitLab OAuth) | The callback is where GitLab redirects the browser back; only cookies can travel on that redirect. Without the cookie Coder answers `303 /login`. |
| Built-in web terminal `/@user/ws.agent/terminal` | Dashboard SPA; its fetch/WebSocket calls use the cookie only (not needed if you use ttyd as an app) |

A browser only accepts a host-only cookie from a response **served by that host**. Middle lives on
another host (`middle.example.com`), so it can't set it, and `Domain=` cookies are ruled out (they
would leak the user's Coder token to every workspace app subdomain).

## 2. Solution in one picture

Put a tiny, stateless **bounce** on the plane host via one ALB rule. Middle stays where it is and
keeps all logic.

```mermaid
sequenceDiagram
  participant P as Portal (browser)
  participant M as Middle
  participant A as ALB coder-a.example.com
  participant L as Bounce Lambda (role per plane)
  participant S as AWS STS
  participant C as Coder plane A
  participant G as GitLab
  P->>M: POST /v1/bounce/start {plane, purpose: external-auth, provider} (user JWT)
  M-->>P: {bounce_url, code} — one-time, 60s, bound to user+plane+redirect
  P->>A: form POST code → https://coder-a.example.com/_portal/bounce (same tab)
  A->>L: rule host=coder-a + path=/_portal/* (priority before Coder)
  L->>M: POST /internal/bounce/redeem {code, host, iam: signed GetCallerIdentity}
  M->>S: replay signed request (fixed endpoint)
  S-->>M: arn …:assumed-role/coder-bounce-plane-a/…
  M->>M: role→plane allowlist, consume code, check host, mint 5-min "bounce-a" token
  M-->>L: {session_token, redirect_path, max_age: 300}
  L-->>P: 302 /external-auth/primary-gitlab/callback?redirect=/_portal/done + Set-Cookie (host-only)
  P->>C: callback (cookie) → Coder stores oauth_redirect=/_portal/done
  C-->>P: 307 gitlab.com/oauth/authorize (PKCE + state)
  P->>G: consent
  G-->>P: 302 back to Coder callback ?code=…
  P->>C: callback (cookie) → Coder exchanges code, stores the user's GitLab link
  C-->>P: 307 /_portal/done
  P->>A: GET /_portal/done (cookie)
  A->>L: same rule
  L->>M: POST /internal/bounce/done {host, session_token, iam}
  M->>C: delete that API key (only if it is the "bounce-a" key)
  M-->>L: {redirect_url: portal}
  L-->>P: 302 portal/?linked=1 + Set-Cookie coder_session_token=; Max-Age=0
  P->>M: GET /v1/external-auth/primary-gitlab → authenticated: true
```

## 3. What was proven

### 3.1 Bounce mechanics

| Check | Local lab | AWS lab |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `/_portal/*` on plane host → bounce, everything else → Coder | ALB emulator | Real ALB listener rule (priority 10) → Lambda target group |
| Lambda identity proven to middle | Assumed role via lab IAM user | **Real Lambda execution role** (`assumed-role/coder-bounce-plane-a/coder-bounce-plane-a`) verified with STS |
| 302 + host-only cookie, no `Domain=` | `HttpOnly; SameSite=Lax` (http) | `HttpOnly; Secure; SameSite=Lax` |
| Cookie alone authenticates on the plane host | 200, `/users/me` = alice (401 without cookie) | same |
| No token/code in URLs | form POST; clean address bar | same |
| Replay same code | 403 | 403 |
| Wrong audience (server id) | 403 `audience mismatch` | 403 |
| Caller not the trusted role (plain IAM user) | 403 `caller is not an assumed role` | — |
| Direct call to middle redeem without Lambda | — | 403 |

Observed on AWS: the ALB turns the Lambda's relative `Location` into an absolute URL on the same
host (harmless).

### 3.2 GitLab external auth (real gitlab.com OAuth app, local lab)

| Check | Result |
| --- | --- |
| Portal shows link status from `GET /v1/external-auth/primary-gitlab` | `authenticated: false` → "Connect GitLab"; `true` → "GitLab: linked" |
| Bounce → `/external-auth/primary-gitlab/callback?redirect=%2F_portal%2Fdone` | Coder sets `oauth_redirect=/_portal/done` and 307s to `gitlab.com/oauth/authorize` (PKCE, `scope=write_repository read_api`) |
| GitLab consent → Coder callback with the bounced cookie | Link stored ("You've authenticated with GitLab!" before the `/done` step existed) |
| Coder → `/_portal/done` → portal | Browser lands on the portal, button shows "GitLab: linked" |
| Consent session after `/done` | Bounce key revoked (`revoked=true` in middle log, old token → 401), cookie cleared; `http://coder…/` shows Coder's login, **not the dashboard** |
| Git inside the workspace | `git clone` / push over HTTPS via Coder's GitLab credentials works |
| Unlink (`DELETE /v1/external-auth/primary-gitlab`) and re-link | Works; GitLab auto-approves an app the user already authorized (revoke it in GitLab → Applications to see the consent screen again) |

## 4. Trust model

| Question | Answer |
| --- | --- |
| How does middle know **which user**? | From the **code**, minted while the user was authenticated to middle (IdP JWT). The Lambda never asserts identity. |
| How does middle know the caller is **the bounce Lambda**? | IAM: the Lambda sends a **pre-signed `sts:GetCallerIdentity`**; middle replays it to a **fixed** STS endpoint and gets the role ARN. No shared secret; middle needs no AWS credentials. |
| Why can't plane A's Lambda redeem plane B's codes? | Allowlist maps **role → plane**; code's plane must equal caller's plane and the request host must equal that plane's host. |
| Why can't a captured signature be reused? | Signed headers `X-Middle-Server-Id` (audience) and `X-Middle-Code-Sha256` = sha256(`action` + `\n` + value) — `redeem` + code, or `done` + session token — so a proof is valid for one middle, one action, one value. STS signatures expire (~15 min); codes are single-use, 60 s. |
| What if the code leaks? | It contains no token, is single-use/short-lived, bound to one plane host, and only the trusted Lambda can redeem it. Sent via form POST so it stays out of history and ALB access logs. |
| Open redirect? | Redirect paths are computed by middle (`external-auth` / `terminal` purposes); the Lambda only accepts a same-origin path on redeem, and on `/done` only an http(s) URL returned by middle (`bounce.done_redirect`). |
| Can the consent cookie be used to browse the Coder dashboard? | Only during the consent round trip. It carries a **dedicated 5-minute key (`bounce-<plane>`)**, which `/done` revokes; the cookie itself is cleared. `/internal/bounce/done` only ever deletes keys named `bounce-<plane>`, never the user's main `middle-<plane>` key. |
| What if the user abandons consent mid-way? | The bounce key expires on its own after 5 minutes (cookie `Max-Age` = 300). |

Allowlist matching notes: STS returns `arn:aws:sts::<acct>:assumed-role/<role-name>/<session>` —
match **account + role name** (role path is dropped); optionally pin the role unique id
(`AROA…`, the `UserId` prefix) so a deleted/recreated role isn't trusted automatically.

### 4.1 Tokens and timers (GitLab consent)

| Token | Where it lives | Lifetime | Used for |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| Main `middle-<plane>` key | **Middle only**, never sent to the browser for consent | 24 h, re-minted | Link status (`GET /v1/external-auth/{id}`), unlink, workspaces… — server-side |
| Dedicated `bounce-<plane>` key | The consent cookie on the plane host | 5 min, **revoked at `/_portal/done`** | GitLab consent round trip only |

- The bounce key is minted **at redeem** (when the Lambda redeems the code), not when the user
  clicks; minting deletes any previous `bounce-<plane>` key, so there is at most one per user.
- Two independent timers:

| Timer | Covers | Recommended | Notes |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| One-time code (`code_ttl_seconds`) | Click → form POST to `/_portal/bounce` (sub-second) | 30–60 s | Minted on click ("Connect GitLab"), single use, consumed **before** the GitLab page |
| Consent key + cookie (`bounceConsentTTLSeconds`, 300 s today) | Bounce → GitLab (login, 2FA/SSO, consent) → `/_portal/done` | 5–10 min | Too short and GitLab's redirect back finds no valid cookie → Coder login page, user restarts from the portal. Longer is low-risk: revoked at `/done`, expires if abandoned |

Naming note: the bounce key is `bounce-a`, not `middle-a-bounce` — middle treats `middle-a-*` as
legacy variants of its main key and would delete it when re-minting.

